#[allow(clippy::too_many_lines)]
fn slice_vllm_gemma4_window() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── Rule 1 (category N — dtype + alignment, the tile-SIZING axis):
        // the launch-refusal capacity law — a dtype-default tile at a large
        // head_dim asks for more shared memory than the device has and the
        // LAUNCH REFUSES; the tile must be derived from the capacity. ──
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "head-dim-smem-tile-cap".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (a kernel tile sized by a DTYPE DEFAULT alone launches a shared-memory requirement the device ceiling can refuse outright — a LAUNCH REFUSAL (OutOfResources), never a slowdown; the tile must be derived from the capacity: head-dim cap first, then a halving loop against the measured device smem, occupancy-gated; category N — dtype + alignment): the founding failure is a Triton prefill attention at head_dim 512, fp16: the dtype-default tile (f32 -> 32, sm80+ -> 128, else 64 — no head_dim, no ceiling in the selector) asked 294,912 B of shared memory against the A100's 166,912 B opt-in ceiling and Triton refused the launch with 'OutOfResources: shared memory' — the PR body's fix adds the head-dim cap verbatim: 'if Lk >= 512:' / 'BLOCK = min(BLOCK, 32)' (which alone lands the requirement at 73,728 B). THE ATTRIBUTION CORRECTION this batch verified (plan 303 T2, byte-level): that cap lives on main's triton_prefill_attention.py — NOT in #60289's merged diff (b803edbc9, config-streamline only; the research draft carried the PR-body citation, the diff check re-homed it). THE LANDED MACHINERY on main is the general form, verbatim: 'def _fit_block_to_shared_memory(block: int, head_dim: int, element_size: int) -> int:' with the docstring 'Halve the tile until its Q, K and P tiles fit in shared memory.' and the loop 'while block > 16 and element_size * block * (2 * head_dim + block) > max_shared_mem: block //= 2' — the byte formula is exact (Q tile block x head_dim + K tile block x head_dim + P tile block x block, one element_size factor), the floor is 16, the caller order is cap-then-loop: 'BLOCK = get_block_size(q.dtype)' / 'if Lk >= 512:' / 'BLOCK = min(BLOCK, 32)' / 'BLOCK = _fit_block_to_shared_memory(BLOCK, triton.next_power_of_2(Lk), q.element_size())'. The cap's own honesty comment is load-bearing: 'min() leaves dtypes whose default tile is already 32, such as float32, alone.' — the dtype-default stays authoritative where it already fits (the two-regime shape). THE EXTENSION (#60317, open, head 8f2ae76fd) tiers the tile by the MEASURED device smem instead of a platform guess, verbatim: the reader 'shared_memory_per_block_optin' (falling back to shared_memory_per_block) cached per device, then the ladder — 'Blackwell Datacenter (B100/B200/GB200/B300) with TMEM' by capability family 100; '# Hopper SM90 H100/H200, Ampere SM80 A100/A30, Orin SM87' at 'max_smem >= 160000' (head 256: BLOCK_M 32 / 8 warps / TILE 128; head 512: BLOCK_M 32 / TILE 64); '# Blackwell SM120/121 RTX 5090, Ada SM89 L4/4090, Ampere SM86 3090/A10' at 'max_smem >= 100000' (TILE 64); the small-memory arm 'if max_smem < 96000:' / 'TILE_SIZE_PREFILL = 16' plus 'launch_num_stages = 1'. THE OCCUPANCY GATES are the anti-regime half, in-source: head-512 tuning admits only when 'enough_hd512_ctas' holds — 'q.shape[0] * num_kv_heads >= 2048 or (num_kv_heads == 1 and q.shape[0] * q.shape[1] >= 8192)' — because 'Small shapes below this threshold fallback to default TILE_SIZE=32 to maximize CTA parallelism' (the PR's own measured negative: 0.86-0.99x on B=1 seq=512 shapes, deliberately routed back to the upstream heuristic); a short-query guard 'min_q_for_large_head = max(16, 32 // num_queries_per_kv)' keeps MTP-verify batches (q=4) from padding BLOCK_M=16 to 32 with masked rows, and 'has_decode_tail' excludes mixed decode-tail batches from the head-512 4-warp arm. DECISION CONTENT: (a) the failure mode is BINARY — a launch that never starts (OutOfResources) reads as zero throughput; capacity validation is a correctness precondition, not a tuning knob; (b) TWO-REGIME: the dtype default stays the answer where capacity is ample (the f32 arm) — the derivation only ever NARROWS; (c) the cap-then-loop order is load-bearing: the analytic head-dim cap removes the known-bad region before the byte loop measures the rest; (d) the byte formula is the kernel's OWN tile geometry (2*head_dim + block), not a constant — a different staging plan re-derives it; (e) occupancy and query-shape gates bound the tuned arm — the capacity-derived tile is admitted only where the grid can still fill the device (the measured small-shape negative is part of the rule, not an embarrassment). REGIME: attention kernels at head_dim >= 256 (512-class global/SWA heads), any backend whose tile is selected host-side from dtype defaults; JIT-compiled kernels where the failure surfaces as OutOfResources instead of a perf cliff. ANTI-REGIME: small head_dims (the default fits, the derivation is dead code); runtimes that auto-spill smem to local memory (a slowdown, different corpus); kernels whose smem is a fixed constant independent of head_dim. KINSHIP (do not double-count): shared-mem-aware-tile (the thin seed-era kin — sizing 'aware of' smem without the capacity law, the launch-refusal class, or the two-regime cap; cite, do not ride); dynamic-shared-memory-runtime-sized owns launch-time smem SIZING for a runtime dimension (making the allocation match head_dim — this sizes the TILE against a fixed ceiling; compose: size the tile, then size the dynamic smem to it); hardcoded-fast-path-jit-specialization owns config MATCHING (this is capacity DERIVATION); vector-width-admission-checks-every-outer-stride (Batch 296, same category) is the admission-predicate sibling — that validates a WIDTH against an alignment ceiling, this DERIVES a TILE against a capacity ceiling. GREP EVIDENCE (rule-id level over both corpora, 3+ vocabularies): 'smem|shared' -> staging/reduction/broadcast families (dequant-once, dsmem-cluster, warp-reduce) — no tile-capacity owner; 'tile.*(cap|fit|budget|ceil)' -> ZERO at rule-id level; 'head.?dim|head_size' -> the GQA/attention geometry families — no sizing owner. NotBenchable here (the failure is a refused launch — a correctness axis no Pareto bench hosts; the byte arithmetic, the floor and the cap are CPU-testable and ARE the fixtures). Source: vllm-project/vllm, Apache-2.0 — main triton_prefill_attention.py get_block_size + _fit_block_to_shared_memory + context_attention_fwd (cap at 'if Lk >= 512:', the float32-alone comment) at the #60289 merge pin b803edbc9; PR #60289 body (the OutOfResources row 294,912 B / 166,912 B + the min(BLOCK, 32) fix); PR #60317 head 8f2ae76fd22ecc81733857e8da51e67e312c62a1 triton_unified_attention.py (_get_device_max_smem, the SMEM tier ladder, enough_hd512_ctas, min_q_for_large_head, has_decode_tail, the 0.86-0.99x small-shape fallback). Batch 297 / Plan 303 / Research 338.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// ref_shape: the tile comes from the DTYPE default alone — the\n// launch's shared-memory requirement is never checked against the\n// device ceiling, so a large head_dim asks for more smem than the\n// platform has and the LAUNCH REFUSES (OutOfResources), not slows.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n/// the dtype-default ladder (the source's get_block_size): f32 -> 32,\n/// sm80+ -> 128, else 64 — no head_dim, no ceiling in the selector.\nfn get_block_size(element_size: usize) -> usize {\n    if element_size == 4 { 32 } else { 128 }\n}\n\n/// bytes of shared memory one (BLOCK x head_dim) Q tile + one (BLOCK x\n/// head_dim) K tile + one (BLOCK x BLOCK) P tile require — the source's\n/// own element_size * block * (2 * head_dim + block) form.\nfn smem_bytes(block: usize, head_dim: usize, element_size: usize) -> usize {\n    element_size * block * (2 * head_dim + block)\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_dtype_default_refuses_at_head_dim_512() {\n    // fp16 (2 B), default block 128, head_dim 512: the founding failure —\n    // 294,912 B requested against the A100's 166,912 B opt-in ceiling.\n    let block = get_block_size(2);\n    let req = smem_bytes(block, 512, 2);\n    assert_eq!(req, 294_912);\n    let max_shared_mem = 166_912u64;\n    assert!(\n        req as u64 > max_shared_mem,\n        \"the launch must refuse: OutOfResources: shared memory\"\n    );\n    // the same default is fine at head_dim 128 — the failure is\n    // head_dim-shaped, which the dtype axis never sees.\n    assert!(smem_bytes(block, 128, 2) as u64 <= max_shared_mem);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn f32_default_already_fits_at_512() {\n    // the honesty arm: f32's default tile is 32, and 32 rows fit —\n    // the dtype default stays authoritative there.\n    let block = get_block_size(4);\n    assert_eq!(block, 32);\n    assert!(smem_bytes(block, 512, 4) as u64 <= 166_912);\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// opt_pattern: the tile is DERIVED from the capacity -- the analytic\n// head-dim cap first (min(BLOCK, 32) at head_dim >= 512), then a halving\n// loop against the device ceiling with a hard floor of 16; where the\n// dtype default already fits it is left alone (the two-regime rule).\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nfn get_block_size(element_size: usize) -> usize {\n    if element_size == 4 { 32 } else { 128 }\n}\n\nfn smem_bytes(block: usize, head_dim: usize, element_size: usize) -> usize {\n    element_size * block * (2 * head_dim + block)\n}\n\nfn fit_block_to_shared_memory(\n    mut block: usize,\n    head_dim: usize,\n    element_size: usize,\n    max_shared_mem: u64,\n) -> usize {\n    if head_dim >= 512 {\n        block = block.min(32); // the head-dim cap: min(BLOCK, 32)\n    }\n    // halve until the tiles fit (floor 16) -- the capacity loop\n    while block > 16 && smem_bytes(block, head_dim, element_size) as u64 > max_shared_mem {\n        block /= 2;\n    }\n    block\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_cap_alone_lands_inside_the_ceiling_at_512() {\n    // fp16: 128 -> capped to 32 -> 67,584 B, inside the A100's 166,912 --\n    // the loop never even fires (the founding failure is a cap fix).\n    let max_shared_mem = 166_912u64;\n    let block = fit_block_to_shared_memory(get_block_size(2), 512, 2, max_shared_mem);\n    assert_eq!(block, 32);\n    assert_eq!(smem_bytes(block, 512, 2), 67_584);\n    assert!(smem_bytes(block, 512, 2) as u64 <= max_shared_mem);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_loop_halves_and_lands_inside_the_ceiling() {\n    // fp32 at head 512 against a 100 KiB ceiling: the 32-row tile busts\n    // (135,168), the loop walks 32 -> 16 (66,560, inside) and stops.\n    let ceiling = 100_000u64;\n    let block = fit_block_to_shared_memory(get_block_size(4), 512, 4, ceiling);\n    assert_eq!(block, 16);\n    assert_eq!(smem_bytes(block, 512, 4), 66_560);\n    assert!(smem_bytes(block, 512, 4) as u64 <= ceiling);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_floor_semantics_may_still_refuse_a_tiny_device() {\n    // the loop's floor is 16 (the source's `while block > 16`): on an\n    // absurd 64 KiB device the 16-row tile STILL busts -- the derivation\n    // narrows as far as the floor allows; capping stops, it never\n    // eliminates. Honest floor semantics, asserted as such.\n    let tiny = 65_536u64;\n    let block = fit_block_to_shared_memory(get_block_size(4), 512, 4, tiny);\n    assert_eq!(block, 16);\n    assert!(smem_bytes(block, 512, 4) as u64 > tiny);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_f32_default_is_left_alone_where_it_fits() {\n    // the source's own comment: min() leaves dtypes whose default tile\n    // is already 32, such as float32, alone -- the two-regime arm,\n    // byte-identical.\n    let max_shared_mem = 166_912u64;\n    let block = fit_block_to_shared_memory(get_block_size(4), 512, 4, max_shared_mem);\n    assert_eq!(block, 32);\n    assert!(smem_bytes(block, 512, 4) as u64 <= max_shared_mem);\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        // ── Rule 2 (category F — flash-attn tiling, the scan-BOUNDS axis):
        // the SWA evicted-prefix analytic skip — the first in-window tile is
        // derived from the window algebra, zero mask reads, static per shape,
        // with the zero-length clamp as the totality guarantee. ──
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "swa-evicted-prefix-tile-analytic-skip".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (a sliding-window attention scan derives its FIRST in-window tile analytically from the window algebra — start_tile = max(0, (seq_len - q_len - window + 1) // TILE) — so the loop never touches evicted prefix tiles; the active count is CLAMPED at zero so a degenerate or inconsistent feed cannot drive the segment decomposition negative; category F — flash-attn tiling): the naive form scans every KV tile of the sequence and per-key window masking discards the evicted prefix after the fact — the scan pays for whole tiles the algebra already excludes, and at long context the ratio is the window over the sequence. THE DERIVATION, verbatim (PR #60317 head 8f2ae76fd, triton_unified_attention.py, kernel side): 'start_tile_idx = tl.maximum(0, (seq_len - cur_batch_query_len - SLIDING_WINDOW + 1) // TILE_SIZE)' — the earliest key any query row can see is seq_len - q_len - window + 1 (the first query row sits at seq_len - q_len and carries window - 1 of lookback), so every tile below that index is fully evicted and provably zero-contribution; 'active_tiles = tl.maximum(0, num_tiles - start_tile_idx)' and 'tiles_per_segment = tl.maximum(1, cdiv_fn(active_tiles, NUM_SEGMENTS_PER_SEQ))'. THE ADMISSION PREDICATE is explicit — 'CAN_CLAMP_3D_SWA' requires the Gemma4-SWA signature (head_size 256, window 512 or 1024), 'SLIDING_WINDOW > 0', 'CHUNK_LOOKBACK == -1', causal, and NOT (per-seq causal or mm-prefix or rswa): the clamp must never fire where another mask semantic already governs the same tiles. THE DEGENERATE FEED is the correctness half: 'active_tiles = tl.maximum(0, num_tiles - start_tile_idx)' keeps tiles_per_segment >= 1 when an inconsistent batch feed (or the signed-i32 kernel arithmetic) would drive the raw difference negative — the source's own framing is that the clamp 'prevents uninitialized reads'; the per-segment early return 'if segm_start_tile >= num_tiles: return' bounds every segment against the same num_tiles. THE REDUCE TWIN carries the same derivation on the segmented-softmax side (reduce_segments recomputes start_tile_idx from the batch offsets) and adds the all-invalid guard 'overall_max = tl.where(overall_max > float(\"-inf\"), overall_max, 0.0)' so a fully-masked reduction cannot propagate -inf into the rescale. THE OCCUPANCY SWITCH is the posture half: the 3D segmented decode stays for small batches and the kernel drops to 2D once 'num_seqs * num_kv_heads >= 128' AND 'sliding_window_val < max_seqlen_k' — the clamped segments stop paying for themselves when the 2D grid alone can fill the device. DECISION CONTENT: (a) the bound is ANALYTIC — zero mask reads, static per (seq_len, q_len, window, TILE) shape, computable at launch; a mask-driven skip reads bytes to learn what the algebra already knows; (b) the derivation is the launch-time FIRST index, not a mid-loop break — the loop domain itself shrinks, which is what the segmented reduction and the grid sizing inherit; (c) the clamp is the totality guarantee: max(0, .) on the tile start and max(0, .) on the active count make the decomposition total over inconsistent feeds — a negative tile count would launch garbage segments and read uninitialized scratch; (d) the admission predicate is conjunction-strict (SWA signature + causal + no chunked lookback + no competing mask semantics) — an analytic skip that fires under another mask's authority is a correctness bug, not an optimization; (e) the posture switch rides the same algebra: when the active window no longer spans enough of the sequence to matter (window >= seq) the clamp is moot and stays off. REGIME: sliding-window attention at long context (the naive scan's cost ratio is seq/window — the founding 4.29x SWA-decode claim is source-reported on A100, provenance never a bar); prefill AND decode (the derivation carries both; the decode twin lives in reduce_segments). ANTI-REGIME: full attention (no evicted prefix to derive past); chunked/sliding-lookback KV semantics (CHUNK_LOOKBACK != -1 — the window algebra no longer bounds the scan); mask-driven attention where a mask tensor governs visibility (derive nothing — the mask IS the truth); seq_len <= window (the clamp never fires; the loop was already minimal). KINSHIP (do not double-count): mask-block-classification-skip-placement owns the DATA-DRIVEN twin — it READS mask bytes to classify each (query-block, k-block) tile fully-masked/all-zero/mixed and skips on the read; THIS derives the bound from the window algebra with zero reads; the two can compose (an analytic start narrows the domain a classifier then walks). query-blocked-prefill-score-span-bound (Batch 275) owns the HOST-side twin — blocking the QUERY axis into blocks whose visible key span is computed from the same window floor (historyCount + offset + 1 - window); THIS moves the bound INTO the kernel's tile loop and shaves the scan, not the dispatch; chain-rows-attend-own-keys-in-prefix-per-row-key-bound owns the per-row key bound for chain decode rounds (dispatch elimination — the tail kernel dies); cost-model-counts-work-the-kernel-skips owns the COST-MODEL axis (what a ceiling should CHARGE a windowed kernel — this is the skip mechanism the cost model prices); ancestor-mask-bit-visibility-block-skip-tree-attention owns tree-attention visibility (parent-chain semantics, not window algebra). GREP EVIDENCE (rule-id level): 'window|skip|span|bound' hits route to the five kin named above + the ring/lifecycle family (mirror-ring-store, kv-window-device-resident, draft-context-ring — storage, not scan bounds); 'start_tile|evicted' -> ZERO; 'analytic' hits are the backward-derivative and construction-identity families — no scan-bounds owner. NotBenchable here (the win axis is tiles-scanned, a CPU-countable quantity — the fixtures ARE the bench; no Pareto axis in this corpus hosts it). Source: vllm-project/vllm PR #60317 head 8f2ae76fd22ecc81733857e8da51e67e312c62a1 (Apache-2.0) — triton_unified_attention.py (the IS_3D derivation + clamp, the CAN_CLAMP_3D_SWA constexpr + host predicate, the 3D->2D switch at num_seqs * num_kv_heads >= 128, reduce_segments twin + the overall_max guard) + triton_attention_helpers.py (compute_tile_loop_bounds start_tile_idx threading). Batch 297 / Plan 303 / Research 338.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// ref_shape: the tile loop runs over EVERY KV tile of the sequence\n// and per-key window masking discards the evicted prefix after the\n// fact - the scan pays for whole tiles the window algebra already\n// excludes, and nothing clamps the segment count on a degenerate feed.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nconst TILE: usize = 16;\n\nfn scanned_tiles_naive(seq_len: usize) -> usize {\n    seq_len.div_ceil(TILE) // every tile visited; the mask filters later\n}\n\nfn visited_keys_naive(seq_len: usize, q_len: usize, window: usize) -> usize {\n    let mut visited = 0usize;\n    for q in 0..q_len {\n        let key_hi = seq_len - q_len + q; // causal: this query's own position\n        let key_lo = key_hi.saturating_sub(window.saturating_sub(1));\n        visited += key_hi - key_lo + 1;\n    }\n    visited\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_full_scan_pays_for_evicted_prefix_tiles() {\n    // seq 8192, 512 query rows, window 512: the mask admits 512*512 keys\n    // but the scan walks all 512 tiles - 480 of them pure eviction.\n    let (seq_len, q_len, window) = (8192usize, 512usize, 512usize);\n    assert_eq!(visited_keys_naive(seq_len, q_len, window), q_len * window);\n    assert_eq!(scanned_tiles_naive(seq_len), 512);\n    assert_eq!((seq_len - window) / TILE, 480);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_naive_segment_count_has_no_totality_guarantee() {\n    // an inconsistent feed (seq_len < q_len - a malformed batch) drives\n    // the raw first-key expression NEGATIVE in signed arithmetic; the\n    // naive form has no clamp, so the segment decomposition inherits the\n    // garbage (the uninitialized-read hazard).\n    let raw_first_key = 4i32 - 16 - 512 + 1; // seq - q - window + 1\n    assert_eq!(raw_first_key, -523);\n    // the cast is parenthesized: bare `TILE as i32 < 0` parses `<` as\n    // the start of generic arguments, the parse defect this class is.\n    assert!(raw_first_key / (TILE as i32) < 0);\n}\n\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// opt_pattern: the first in-window tile is DERIVED analytically -\n// start = max(0, (seq - q - window + 1) / TILE) - the active count is\n// CLAMPED at zero, and every segment is bounded by num_tiles; the loop\n// domain itself shrinks and a degenerate feed returns early, total.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nconst TILE: usize = 16;\n\nfn start_tile(seq_len: usize, q_len: usize, window: usize) -> usize {\n    // max(0, (seq - q - window + 1)) / TILE - the literal source form:\n    // signed arithmetic, then the clamp, exactly as tl.maximum does.\n    let raw = seq_len as i64 - q_len as i64 - window as i64 + 1;\n    (raw.max(0) as u64 as usize) / TILE\n}\n\nfn active_tiles(seq_len: usize, q_len: usize, window: usize) -> usize {\n    let num_tiles = seq_len.div_ceil(TILE);\n    num_tiles.saturating_sub(start_tile(seq_len, q_len, window))\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_analytic_bound_equals_the_masked_truth() {\n    // the first key any query row sees is 8192 - 512 - 512 + 1 = 7169,\n    // tile 448: exactly the tiles the mask would have kept.\n    let (seq_len, q_len, window) = (8192usize, 512usize, 512usize);\n    assert_eq!(start_tile(seq_len, q_len, window), 448);\n    assert_eq!(active_tiles(seq_len, q_len, window), 512 - 448);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_scan_domain_shrinks_eight_fold() {\n    let (seq_len, q_len, window) = (8192usize, 512usize, 512usize);\n    let before = seq_len.div_ceil(TILE);\n    let after = active_tiles(seq_len, q_len, window);\n    assert_eq!(before, 512);\n    assert_eq!(after, 64);\n    assert_eq!(before / after, 8);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_clamp_totals_the_degenerate_feed() {\n    // the inconsistent feed: the raw first key is negative, the clamp\n    // parks the start at 0, the active count stays the whole sequence -\n    // total, never negative, never garbage.\n    let (seq_len, q_len, window) = (4usize, 16usize, 512usize);\n    assert_eq!(start_tile(seq_len, q_len, window), 0);\n    assert_eq!(active_tiles(seq_len, q_len, window), 1); // ceil(4/16)\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_occupancy_switch_picks_the_posture() {\n    // 3D segmented decode for small batches; 2D once the active CTAs\n    // (num_seqs * num_kv_heads) reach 128 AND the window is shorter\n    // than the sequence.\n    let use_3d = |num_seqs: usize, kv_heads: usize, window: usize, seq: usize| {\n        !(window < seq && num_seqs * kv_heads >= 128)\n    };\n    assert!(use_3d(8, 8, 512, 8192)); // 64 CTAs: keep 3D\n    assert!(!use_3d(32, 4, 512, 8192)); // 128 CTAs: switch to 2D\n    assert!(use_3d(64, 8, 8192, 8192)); // window == seq: clamp is moot\n}\n\n".to_string(),
        },
        // ── Rule 3 (category D — dispatch/host-orchestration, the metadata
        // axis): the pigeonhole construction — when the constraint set
        // implies the output exactly, CONSTRUCT it (one arange) instead of
        // computing it through the general reduction pipeline. ──
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "analytic-constraint-prefix-sum-arange".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (a host-side metadata builder whose constraint set implies its output EXACTLY must CONSTRUCT the known output — one arange — instead of computing it through the general reduction pipeline; the D2H mirror is likewise CONSTRUCTED host-side, deleting the copy; category D — dispatch/host-orchestration): the founding shape is vLLM's KV-sharing fast-prefill decode metadata (PR #60445, head 213591763). The general path, verbatim from the diff's removed lines: 'request_ids = torch.bucketize(logits_indices, query_start_loc[1:], right=True)', a zeros buffer, 'num_decode_tokens.scatter_add_(0, request_ids.to(num_decode_tokens.dtype), torch.ones_like(request_ids))', an empty output with 'decode_query_start_loc[:1].fill_(0)  # Avoid sync from scalar assignment.', 'decode_query_start_loc[1:] = torch.cumsum(num_decode_tokens, dim=0)', and the CPU mirror 'decode_query_start_loc.to(\"cpu\", non_blocking=True)' — six device ops plus a device-to-host transfer to discover a vector the scheduler already knew. THE PIGEHOLE, verbatim from the replacement: 'if common_attn_metadata.max_logits_per_req == 1 and num_logits_indices == num_reqs:' implies every request contributes exactly one decode token, so query_start_loc IS the unit-step range — 'decode_query_start_loc = torch.arange(0, num_reqs + 1, device=query_start_loc.device, dtype=query_start_loc.dtype)', the mirror constructed host-side 'decode_query_start_loc_cpu = torch.arange(0, num_reqs + 1, device=\"cpu\", dtype=query_start_loc.dtype)', 'decode_max_query_len = 1', 'total_num_decode_tokens = num_reqs'. ONE arange replaces the bucketize/zeros/scatter_add_/empty/fill_/cumsum pipeline AND the PCIe transfer disappears (nothing to copy — the host builds its own). THE TWO-REGIME is kept honest: the else branch retains the general pipeline verbatim for multi-logit requests (speculative decode), and the PR's measured table shows the fallback at 1.00x parity — the construction never taxes the regime it does not own; the fast arm measured 4.22-5.28x on the metadata builder (~0.17 ms/prefill step, A100 — source-reported, provenance never a bar) and 5.33-5.67x on pure-decode prep. DECISION CONTENT: (a) a constraint that pins the output to a CLOSED FORM is an obligation to construct, not a hint to specialize — the pigeonhole (every request exactly one token) is checked on Python ints the scheduler already holds, before any device op is launched; (b) the mirror half is part of the law: a CPU twin of a device tensor that the constraint also pins should be CONSTRUCTED on the host, not computed on the device and copied — the copy is the hidden seventh op; (c) the fallback is part of the admission, not boilerplate — a construction rule without its general-path parity arm silently corrupts the regime it forgot (multi-logit/speculative feeds); (d) the shapes must agree end-to-end (dtype, device, length n+1) — an arange that drifted from the general path's dtype would fork the consumer's indexing; (e) op-count, not bytes, is the win axis — six launches and a sync collapse to one host-side conditional and one cheap kernel. REGIME: per-step scheduler/metadata builders over ragged batches where a fast-path flag (here max_logits_per_req == 1) pins the shape; any prefix-sum/cumsum-of-ones structure whose inputs are an identity under a held constraint. ANTI-REGIME: multi-token or speculative feeds (the constraint fails — general path); constraints over DATA still on device (checking them would cost the sync the construction saves); outputs with data-dependent values the constraint does not pin. KINSHIP (do not double-count): lm-head-loss-positions-only owns dispatch ELISION under a consumer-set predicate (skipping work nobody consumes — this CONSTRUCTS work that is known); construction-identity-row-classes-replace-probe owns construction-vs-probing for EQUIVALENCE CLASSES of rows (a table that must hold by construction — different object: classes, not a closed-form output); csr-index-via-prefix-sum owns prefix-sum MECHANICS for index construction (this eliminates the prefix sum); predicate-gated-skip-empty-workset-step owns skip guards on empty worksets (adjacent admission shape, different axis). GREP EVIDENCE (rule-id level over both corpora): 'arange|pigeonhole' -> ZERO; 'cumsum' -> chunk-reset-cumsum, cumsum-additive-bias, cumsum-gate-to-decay-matrix, fuse-gate-transform-chunk-cumsum, grouped-launch-cumsum-offsets — all reduction mechanics, no elision-by-pigeonhole owner; 'prefix-sum' -> csr-index-via-prefix-sum (mechanics, named above); 'identity' -> degenerate-selection-falls-back-to-identity-uniform + construction-identity-row-classes (both named). NotBenchable here (the win is device ops + a sync removed — a count axis the fixtures pin exactly; no Pareto bench hosts it). Source: vllm-project/vllm PR #60445 head 213591763655c5caf336fd208a4bef6498c51b5a (Apache-2.0) — vllm/v1/attention/backends/utils.py make_kv_sharing_fast_prefill_common_attn_metadata (the removed pipeline + the replacement if/else, both halves verbatim in the diff). Batch 297 / Plan 303 / Research 338.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// ref_shape: the decode metadata is COMPUTED through the general\n// reduction pipeline — bucketize, scatter-add, cumsum — six device\n// ops plus a device-to-host copy, even when the scheduler's own\n// constraint already pins the output to the unit-step range.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\n/// the general path: request id per logit, counts per request, prefix\n/// sum back to query_start_loc — plus the D2H copy the CPU half reads.\n/// Returns (start_locs, device_ops, d2h_copies).\nfn decode_query_start_loc_general(\n    logits_indices: &[u32],\n    num_reqs: usize,\n) -> (Vec<u32>, usize, usize) {\n    let mut ops = 0usize;\n    // bucketize: which request produced each logit\n    let mut request_ids = vec![0u32; logits_indices.len()];\n    for (ri, &li) in request_ids.iter_mut().zip(logits_indices) {\n        *ri = (li as usize).min(num_reqs - 1) as u32;\n    }\n    ops += 1;\n    // zeros + scatter_add into a preallocated count buffer\n    let mut counts = vec![0u32; num_reqs];\n    for &r in &request_ids {\n        counts[r as usize] += 1;\n    }\n    ops += 2;\n    // empty + fill + cumsum into the output\n    let mut qsl = vec![0u32; num_reqs + 1];\n    for i in 0..num_reqs {\n        qsl[i + 1] = qsl[i] + counts[i];\n    }\n    ops += 3;\n    let _cpu = qsl.clone(); // the D2H mirror: copied, not constructed\n    (qsl, ops, 1)\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_general_pipeline_costs_six_ops_and_a_copy() {\n    // every request samples exactly one token: the logits indices are\n    // already the identity — and the pipeline still pays in full.\n    let (qsl, ops, copies) = decode_query_start_loc_general(&[0, 1, 2, 3], 4);\n    assert_eq!(qsl, vec![0, 1, 2, 3, 4]);\n    assert_eq!(ops, 6);\n    assert_eq!(copies, 1);\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// opt_pattern: the pigeonhole check first — one logit per request\n// AND logits == reqs implies query_start_loc IS arange(0, n+1):\n// constructed on device AND host directly, zero copies, one op. The\n// general pipeline remains the fallback for multi-token requests.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nfn decode_query_start_loc(\n    max_logits_per_req: u32,\n    num_logits: usize,\n    num_reqs: usize,\n) -> (Vec<u32>, usize, usize) {\n    if max_logits_per_req == 1 && num_logits == num_reqs {\n        // the constraint implies the output: construct, never compute.\n        // the host twin is built host-side — no D2H copy exists.\n        (Vec::from_iter(0..=num_reqs as u32), 1, 0)\n    } else {\n        // multi-logit (speculative decode): the general pipeline, at\n        // measured parity — the construction never taxes this regime.\n        let per_req = num_logits / num_reqs.max(1);\n        let mut qsl = Vec::with_capacity(num_reqs + 1);\n        qsl.push(0);\n        for i in 0..num_reqs {\n            qsl.push(qsl[i] + per_req as u32);\n        }\n        (qsl, 6, 1)\n    }\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_unit_constraint_constructs_one_arange() {\n    let (qsl, ops, copies) = decode_query_start_loc(1, 4, 4);\n    assert_eq!(qsl, vec![0, 1, 2, 3, 4]);\n    assert_eq!(ops, 1); // was 6\n    assert_eq!(copies, 0); // the D2H copy is gone\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_multi_token_fallback_keeps_the_general_path() {\n    // speculative decode: 2 logits per request — the pigeonhole does\n    // not hold, the fallback computes.\n    let (qsl, ops, copies) = decode_query_start_loc(2, 8, 4);\n    assert_eq!(qsl, vec![0, 2, 4, 6, 8]);\n    assert_eq!(ops, 6);\n    assert_eq!(copies, 1);\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
        // ── Rule 4 (category H — MoE dispatch + backward, the tile-DERIVATION
        // axis): the MoE routed tile ladders from the routing arithmetic —
        // tokens_per_expert = M*topk/E sizes BLOCK_M, and GROUP_M collapses
        // to 1 on scattered streams. ──
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "moe-tokens-per-expert-block-m-cap".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "MECHANISM (a routed-MoE GEMM's M-tile derives from the ROUTING ARITHMETIC — tokens_per_expert = M*top_k/E — not from a fixed wide default: the per-expert bucket a sparse route delivers is exactly what the tile must cover, and GROUP_SIZE_M collapses to 1 while a bucket is smaller than two tiles; category H — MoE dispatch): the naive form runs every expert bucket at one fixed wide BLOCK_M, so a route that delivers 8 rows to each of 128 experts under M=128/top_k=8 pays a 64-row tile for an 8-row bucket — 8x the MMA rows, all padding. THE DERIVATION, verbatim (PR #60504, head 3baa3d4fc, fused_moe.py): 'tokens_per_expert = (M * topk) // max(E, 1)' then the ladder — 'if E >= 64:' / 'block_m = 16 if tokens_per_expert <= 8 else (32 if tokens_per_expert <= 16 else 64)' / 'block_m = max(16, min(block_m, block_shape[0]))' / 'group_m = 1 if tokens_per_expert < 2 * block_m else 32'. Every clause is a decision: the ladder keeps the tile within ~2x of the actual bucket (padding bounded by the rung, never the default), the clamp keeps the tile inside the quantization block_shape contract (a block-quant kernel never sees a tile wider than its own block), and the group collapse encodes the L2 reality of scattered token streams — fewer than two tiles per bucket means consecutive-M grouping has no reuse to chase, so GROUP_M = 1 instead of the dense-GEMM 32. The second tuned variant in the same file ladders the OTHER axes off the same arithmetic — 'block_n = 64 if (tokens_per_expert <= 8 or N <= 176) else 128', 'block_k = 64 if N < 128 else (128 if dtype == \"fp8_w8a8\" or tokens_per_expert <= 8 else 64)', 'group_m = 16 if tokens_per_expert > 128 else 1' — the routing quotient prices the whole tile shape, not one axis. THE SPILL CLIFF is the boundary condition and it is OWNED ELSEWHERE: the PR's headline 19.59x row is really the register cliff — a BLOCK_M=64 x BLOCK_N=128 accumulator at 4 warps exceeds the 255-register budget and spills to local memory (4.865 -> 0.250 ms at TP8 M=128, source-reported) — and that mechanism (a per-thread budget squeeze on a tiled MMA body forcing the accumulator set to spill) is corpus law at spill-fragility-law-accumulator-regcap-cliff; the ladder here is the AVOIDANCE posture (stay at a rung whose accumulator fits), cite the cliff law, never re-own it. DECISION CONTENT: (a) the routing quotient M*top_k/E is the EXPECTED bucket — the tile sizes to the expectation, and the ladder's rungs bound the padding (at most one rung of waste per bucket, by construction); (b) GROUP_M follows the same quotient: scattered streams (bucket < 2 tiles) get 1, dense buckets get the grouped schedule — the dense-GEMM default is a wrong answer on routed work; (c) the block_shape clamp is the quantization contract — the tile never exceeds the block the dequant reads; (d) the small-E regime branches separately (E < 64: block_m from M directly) — the arithmetic only prices routing when there IS routing density; (e) the cliff boundary is a corpus cross-reference: any rung whose accumulator set busts the register budget is refused by the spill law, not tuned around. REGIME: fused/triton MoE GEMMs over routed tokens (bucket-per-expert), autotuned or hand-pinned tile tables; especially serving stacks whose top_k/E ratio moves per model (the ladder re-prices per shape, a pinned table rots). ANTI-REGIME: dense GEMM (no routing quotient exists); single-expert or top_k=1 shards at large batch (the bucket IS the batch — the dense ladder is right); kernels already consuming real per-expert counts (a histogram beats the expectation — this law is for the expectation-priced form). KINSHIP (do not double-count): expert-remainder-routed-to-smaller-tile-twins owns the bucket-REMAINDER axis (the aligned prefix runs the wide twin, the tail runs the smallest covering twin — a work PARTITION at runtime; THIS prices the tile from the expectation up front); expert-member-rows-width-ladder-bit-identical owns the member-rows-per-pass RUNTIME width ladder (measured rungs for the pass width — a different axis from the compile-time tile derivation); expert-major-tile-order-token-tile-cache-resident owns the tile ORDER (expert-major scheduling for cache residency); moe-lone-tile-register-gemv-uniform-tile-kind owns the tiny-bucket tail (<= 2 rows: a lane-per-column GEMV tile kind); spill-fragility-law-accumulator-regcap-cliff owns the register cliff this ladder avoids (CITED above). GREP EVIDENCE (rule-id level over both corpora): 'moe|expert' -> the dispatch/routing/backward families (sentinel-id-skip, unverified-rows-route-zero, lone-tile-gemv, remainder-twins, member-rows-ladder, expert-major-order, indirect-device-dispatch-tile-map) — no tile-derivation owner; 'tokens_per_expert|routing.*tile' -> ZERO; 'group_m|block_m' -> ZERO at rule-id level; 'spill' -> the regcap cliff + kdepth-asymmetry (the cited owner). NotBenchable here (the win axis is padding rows + spill avoidance, both CPU-countable from the routing arithmetic — the fixtures pin them; no Pareto bench hosts it). Source: vllm-project/vllm PR #60504 head 3baa3d4fc0441ee7f890c8d0cf065d91f7de111a (Apache-2.0) — vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/fused_moe.py (the E >= 64 ladder + clamp + group collapse, the second variant's block_n/block_k/group_m, both verbatim in the diff); the spill row from the PR body (4.865 -> 0.250 ms, TP8 M=128, source-reported). Batch 297 / Plan 303 / Research 338. RIDER (Batch 320 / Plan 320 — vllm-project/vllm @ 2bfb8f1 #64467, Apache-2.0 — the PREPARE-KERNEL INSTANCE face, the ladder family's second kernel): this rule keys the ROUTED GEMM's tile on the per-expert quotient M*topk/E; the prepare pass that quant-pads the routed inputs ladders its row tile on TOTAL tokens — verbatim [in-diff] vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/prepare_megamoe.py:611-613 '# On GB200, 8-row tiles win from 64 tokens and 32-row tiles from 1024 (1.3-1.6x over 8 rows); smaller batches stay untiled.' + 'block_m = 32 if num_tokens >= 1024 else 8 if num_tokens >= 64 else 1' (replacing 'block_m = 8 if num_tokens >= 64 else 1'); the just-past-threshold point joins the bitwise-exactness test's parametrize [in-diff] tests/kernels/moe/test_deep_gemm_mega_moe.py:706 '... 434, 1025, 16384'. Compose-never-merge: different kernel (quant/pad prepare, not the routed GEMM), different metric (total num_tokens, not M*topk/E), no clamp/group axis — the rung-ladder SHAPE is the family tie; the 1.3-1.6x figure is an in-comment author claim (no bench artifacts; Co-authored-by Claude Opus 5.5 — provenance weight carried). Batch 320 / Plan 320.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// ref_shape: the tile shape is a FIXED wide default — every expert\n// bucket, however few tokens routing actually sent it, runs the same\n// 64-row M tile — so a bucket of 8 rows burns 56 zero-column MMA rows,\n// and the wide accumulator walks straight at the register cliff.\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nconst BLOCK_M: usize = 64;\n\n/// rows of MMA work one expert bucket costs at the fixed tile\nfn mma_rows_fixed(tokens_per_expert: usize) -> usize {\n    tokens_per_expert.div_ceil(BLOCK_M) * BLOCK_M\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_fixed_tile_burns_padding_on_small_buckets() {\n    // M=128 tokens, top_k=8, E=128 experts: routing sends each expert\n    // 8 rows on average; the 64-row tile runs 8x the useful rows.\n    let (m, top_k, experts) = (128usize, 8usize, 128usize);\n    let tpe = m * top_k / experts;\n    assert_eq!(tpe, 8);\n    assert_eq!(mma_rows_fixed(tpe), 64); // 56 of 64 rows are padding\n    assert_eq!(mma_rows_fixed(tpe) / tpe, 8);\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// opt_pattern: the tile derives from the ROUTING arithmetic —\n// tokens_per_expert = M*topk/E ladders the M tile (16/32/64), clamped\n// into the block_shape contract, and GROUP_M collapses to 1 while the\n// per-expert stream is smaller than two tiles (scattered streams have\n// no L2 reuse to chase).\n#![allow(dead_code)]\n\nfn block_m_for(tokens_per_expert: usize, block_shape_m: usize) -> usize {\n    let ladder = if tokens_per_expert <= 8 {\n        16\n    } else if tokens_per_expert <= 16 {\n        32\n    } else {\n        64\n    };\n    ladder.clamp(16, block_shape_m)\n}\n\nfn group_m_for(tokens_per_expert: usize, block_m: usize) -> usize {\n    if tokens_per_expert < 2 * block_m { 1 } else { 32 }\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_ladder_tracks_the_routing_arithmetic() {\n    // the founding shape: 8 tokens/expert -> a 16-row tile, 2x padding\n    // instead of 8x.\n    assert_eq!(block_m_for(8, 128), 16);\n    assert_eq!(block_m_for(9, 128), 32);\n    assert_eq!(block_m_for(16, 128), 32);\n    assert_eq!(block_m_for(17, 128), 64);\n    // the block_shape contract clamps: a 64-wide blocked-quant kernel\n    // never sees a tile wider than its own block.\n    assert_eq!(block_m_for(64, 64), 64);\n    assert_eq!(block_m_for(16, 64), 32);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn group_m_collapses_on_scattered_streams() {\n    // fewer than 2 tiles of tokens per bucket: grouping buys nothing,\n    // 1 it is; a dense bucket earns the grouped schedule.\n    assert_eq!(group_m_for(8, 16), 1);\n    assert_eq!(group_m_for(31, 16), 1);\n    assert_eq!(group_m_for(32, 16), 32);\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn the_padded_work_is_bounded_by_one_rung() {\n    // at every rung the waste is < the rung width: 8 rows in a 16 tile\n    // wastes 8; 33 rows in a 64 tile wastes 31 — never the 56-row burn\n    // of the fixed default.\n    for (tpe, bm) in [(8usize, 16usize), (9, 32), (16, 32), (33, 64)] {\n        let tile = block_m_for(tpe, 128);\n        assert_eq!(tile, bm);\n        // padding never fills the tile: every bucket exceeds the previous\n        // rung by construction (the waste is always < the tile width).\n        assert!(tile - tpe < tile);\n    }\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}

